{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792310638,"nonce":"OKdjQUNanyEOab9NigyKjYx8jhk=","title":"Llama 3.1 8B Instruct Q4_K_M crashes with SIGSEGV (exit 139) at \\\"[gen] streaming...\\\" — first forward pass. Null pointer dereference in ...","message":"**Status: MOSTLY FIXED — original diagnosis was wrong. 8B works correctly when given the full 5-arg invocation.**\n\n**Real root cause: argv parsing bug in `nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx`**. When fewer than 5 positional args are given (`model prompt max ctx temp`), `Sys.args()` accesses out-of-bounds → SIGSEGV. All prior \"8B SIGSEGV\" reports in this memory file used short-arg patterns:\n\n- `\"Hi\" 4` (3 args, no ctx, no temp) → SIGSEGV\n- `\"Hi\" 4 0.01` (4 args, no ctx) → SIGSEGV\n- `\"Hi\" 4 4096 0.01` (5 args) → **WORKS**\n\n\n```\nrayzor run Main.hx -- \u003c8B-gguf\u003e \"What is the capital of France?\" 6 4096 0.01\n→ \"The capital of France is Paris\" (6 tokens, 56.3s, 0.107 tok/s, EXIT=0)\n```\n\n1B also coherent on multiple prompts with full args:\n- Paris: 0.82 tok/s\n- Haiku: 1.07 tok/s (\"Here is a haiku about the moon: / Luna's gentle, peaceful face\")\n- Quantum entanglement explanation: 1.07 tok/s, fluent and accurate\n\n\nPatch `Main.hx` (`nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx`) to bounds-check `Sys.args()` access AND set proper defaults when args are short. ~10 LOC, narrowly scoped. Should be done in the next focused commit before any further benchmark or test work uses the short-arg form.\n\n\nThe prior `__reflect_ctor_wrap_759` cascade signature on 8B's stdlib paths (TreeNode, BalancedTree, iterator classes) is **real** and **same class** as `bugs_trap_stub_cascade`. But the cascade fires harmlessly — these classes aren't on the inference hot path. The user-visible SIGSEGV was always the argv bug, not the cascade.\n\n`bugs_trap_stub_cascade` remains its own genuine open bug (per workflow `wdf5z299d` Phase 0: STEP2 of `/tmp/trap_repro/repro.sh` still silently exits after `[build]`), but it does NOT actually block 8B generation. Decoupling those investigations.\n\n\n\n\nModel: `Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct-Q4_K_M.gguf` (4.6 GB) at `~/.cache/huggingface/hub/models--lmstudio-community--Meta-Llama-3-8B-Instruct-GGUF/snapshots/0910a3e69201d274d4fd68e89448114cd78e4c82/`.\n\nCommand:\n```\nrayzor run nue/examples/llama-chat/Main.hx -- \u003c8B-gguf-path\u003e \"Hi\" 2\n```\n\nSequence:\n1. `[load] reading GGUF...` ✓\n2. `[lwt] 1-8` ✓ (all 8 layer/weight tensors loaded)\n3. Tokenizer load (gtb 1-11), specials registered ✓\n4. Chat template wrap ✓\n5. Prompt token IDs match exactly (17 tokens for \"What is the capital of France?\") ✓\n6. `[gen] streaming...` — **EXIT 139 (SIGSEGV)** within ~11s wall time\n\nPeak RSS: 4.87 GB (well under 16 GB M1 Pro). Not OOM.\n\n\n```\n* thread #1, name = 'main', queue = 'com.apple.main-thread',\n  stop reason = EXC_BAD_ACCESS (code=1, address=0x0)\n  frame #0: 0x0000000102f64628\n-\u003e  0x102f64628: ldr    x1, [x0]      ; x0 = 0x0 — null deref\n    0x102f6462c: ldr    x0, [x0, #0x18]\n    0x102f64630: mov    x0, x26\n    0x102f64634: ldr    x0, [x0, #0x40]\n  frame #1: 0x0000000102f7ab64\n  frame #2: 0x0000000102f66364\n  frame #3: rayzor`compiler::codegen::tiered_backend::TieredBackend::execute_function + 3372\n  frame #4: rayzor`rayzor::run_file + 15532\n```\n\nFrames 0-2 are in JIT'd code (no symbol). The trap is at the very first load from a null pointer — classic dereference-of-uninitialised-pointer pattern.\n\n\n| Dimension | 1B (works) | 8B (crashes) |\n|---|---|---|\n| hidden | 2048 | **4096** |\n| layers | 16 | **32** |\n| heads (Q/KV) | 32 / 8 | 32 / 8 (same) |\n| head_dim | 64 | **128** |\n| ffn | 8192 | **14336** |\n| vocab | 128256 | 128256 (same) |\n| ctx | 4096 | 4096 (same) |\n\nBoth heads counts and ctx/vocab match. Only hidden / layers / head_dim / ffn change. Most likely culprits for a shape-specific null deref:\n\n1. **A kernel with a hardcoded shape constant** assuming hidden=2048 or head_dim=64; produces a null/wrong pointer at 4096/128.\n2. **A pool revival path** that returns null on first allocation of a new (dtype, shape) bucket the 1B run never reached.\n3. **A trap-stub on a shape-specific class** that the 1B never instantiated. Adjacent symptom: with `RAYZOR_DUMP_FN_PTRS=1`, the 8B run shows trap-stubs on `haxe.ds.TreeNode.new`, `haxe.ds.BalancedTree.new`, `haxe.iterators.ArrayIterator.new`, `haxe.iterators.ArrayKeyValueIterator.new` PLUS a `[trap-stub:cascade] IrFunctionId(270024) __reflect_ctor_wrap_759 calls failed: [IrFunctionId(270003)]` for `TreeNode`. The 1B run doesn't print these. The reflect-wrapper cascade is the same class of bug workflow `woa9qquvk` is investigating (trap-stub-on-import cascade).\n4. **The view/contiguity contract**: 8B's larger tensors may trip an alignment or contiguity assumption the 1B avoided.\n\n\nThe `TreeNode` / `BalancedTree` `__reflect_ctor_wrap_759` cascade is the strongest single signal — same shape as `bugs_trap_stub_cascade`, `bugs_int64_multivar_stub_replace`, and the WorkerPool/TemperatureSampler import cascade that workflow `woa9qquvk` is fixing.\n\nThe 8B's larger vocab loop (or BPE merges count, or some other stdlib path) probably traverses code that instantiates a `BalancedTree` or iterator class which the 1B never touches. If the `__reflect_ctor_wrap` returns a trap-stub that gets dispatched as if it were a real fn pointer, the result is exactly this null-deref shape.\n\n\n1. **Re-run the 8B bringup on the post-cascade-fix binary**. If the cascade fix resolves the TreeNode reflect-wrapper trap-stub, the SIGSEGV may go away entirely.\n\n2. If still crashing: bisect with `RAYZOR_DEBUG_E0382=1` + add `RAYZOR_DEBUG_INFERENCE=1` (or instrument `LlamaModel.forwardIds` to print per-layer entry/exit). Identify whether the crash is in (a) embedding lookup, (b) attention, (c) FFN, (d) layer norm — each is a different shape and different code path.\n\n3. If the crash is in qmatmul: try `RAYZOR_USE_SDOT=0` to rule out the SDOT path. If `=0` works and `=1` crashes, the SDOT kernel has a shape-specific bug at hidden=4096 or head_dim=128.\n\n4. If still crashing: try the **stashed Phase 1 patch** (per `project_kernel_parallelism_phase2_reverted`) — the audit predicted it would WIN at 8B due to larger per-call work. The shape-specific code path differences may also reveal the trap differently.\n\n\nPre-crash sample: 11.97s wall for prompt prefill + 0 generated tokens = entire crash happens before any token is produced. Cannot characterise tok/s yet.\n\nExpected: 1B is ~0.85 tok/s at 24 tokens. 8B is ~4× more compute → expect ~0.2 tok/s. The Phase 1 kernel parallelism patch from `project_kernel_parallelism_phase2_reverted` is expected to win at this scale.\n\n\n- [[project-trap-stub-cascade-foundation]] (whenever the woa9qquvk fix lands)\n- [[bugs-int64-multivar-stub-replace]] (similar fix pattern)\n- [[bugs-forward-ref-stub-unresolved]] (similar fix pattern)\n- [[project-kernel-parallelism-phase2-reverted]] (stashed patch that may apply here)\n- [[project-llama-chat-first-bench]] (1B baseline)\n\n---\nArchived from Claude memory `bugs_llama_8b_sigsegv_at_gen_start` on 2026-10-18. A record of past work; open items in it may have their own issues.","files":null}]}